{
  "0": "Learning objective: replace the prediction loss with binary focal loss, gamma=1 and equal positive/negative class weights, using the existing samples and masks. Downweighting easy examples may concentrate learning on ranking errors near the top-10 boundary, unlike the unsuccessful margin-logistic trial. Preserve sampling, architecture, scoring, optimizer and training settings. Train one independent model per category across all four datasets.",
  "1": "Interest aggregation: replace the current readout with a masked, normalized exponential average of existing GRU states, using weights proportional to 2^(-age/5), where the latest valid state has age zero. This five-interaction half-life may retain persistent interests while emphasizing recent behavior, with no learned pooling parameters. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU, scoring, loss and optimizer; use one model per category across all four datasets.",
  "2": "Network architecture: apply LayerNorm only to the GRU candidate-state preactivation before tanh, retaining the existing reset/update gate equations, hidden width and depth. Stabilizing candidate activations may improve recurrent optimization without adding another recurrent layer or substantially increasing computation. Preserve embeddings, history policy, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer. Train one independent model per category across all four datasets.",
  "3": "Embedding representation: apply parameter-free RMS normalization to nonpadding input item embeddings before the GRU, using epsilon=1e-6 and keeping padding vectors zero. More consistent input magnitudes may reduce recurrent gate saturation and improve sparse-item representations. Preserve embedding dimensions and tying, candidate scoring, GRU topology, history/readout, loss and optimizer. Train one independent model per category across all four datasets; update the predictor writer consistently."
}
